{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Not one day, etc.</b> The reason for the length of these expressions, and their intent, is according to what we explained in the comment on ״וְאָכַלְתֶּם בָּשָׂר״ — that it is a royal decree obligating them to eat. Now, since there is a person whose craving is satisfied by eating meat on one day, and there is one [whose craving is satisfied] in two, and there is one in five, and there is one in ten, and there is one in twenty — and the person says, ״Behold, I have satisfied my craving״ and eats no more — therefore the royal decree comes upon the one whose craving is satisfied in one day, issuing the warning ״not one day״; and upon the one whose craving is satisfied in two comes the warning ״nor two days״; and so for all of them. And He set a limit for them of thirty days of obligatory eating, so that they would come to loathe it, and it would become repulsive to human nature as a consequence of the craving for it. From now on, whoever refrains from eating it even one day has transgressed a negative commandment and nullified a positive commandment."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:07:45.496261110Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}